  <ArticleProse>
    <div class="hc-article-panel hc-article-panel--parchment">
      <span class="hc-eyebrow" style="color: var(--hc-gold); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; display: block;">In this study</span>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">The black-banner reports are read here as the prophetic-form afterlife of the Abbasid daʿwa. Eight matn families are mapped against the daʿwa's documented organizational machinery, the candidate-identification problem is examined family by family, and the cluster's pro-banner and anti-banner halves are treated as competing acts of revolutionary memory preserved in the same early fitan compilations.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="thesis">Opening Thesis</h2>
    <p>The three preceding chapters traced Mahdī clusters whose central work was theological transformation of political defeat. The Kaysānī cluster converted the failure of Mukhtār's revolt and the death of Muḥammad b. al-Ḥanafiyya into deferred occultation. The Zubayrid cluster converted the killing of Ibn al-Zubayr at the violated House into the eschatological reversal of the swallowed army at al-Bayḍāʾ. The ʿUmar II cluster, structurally different from both, did not transform a defeat but preserved a moment of semantic instability in which the title <em>mahdī</em> was still being negotiated around a successful pious-restorer caliph. The cluster treated here is different again. It is the first layer in which Mahdī expectation becomes the explicit instrument of an organized revolutionary daʿwa that actually succeeded, and then, having succeeded, became dynastic propaganda for the regime it produced.</p>

    <p>The Abbasid revolution of 130–132/747–750 is the decisive break-point. Before it, Mahdī material had collected around figures who had been defeated. After it, Mahdī material collected around a regime that ruled. The Abbasid daʿwa machinery — the secret missionary network in Khurāsān, the call to an unnamed <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad</em>, the seventy initial responders, the twelve naqībs, the black banners and black clothing of the Musawwida — is historically documented in the Sunnī and Shīʿī chronicle tradition (Balādhurī, Ṭabarī, Yaʿqūbī, Masʿūdī, Dīnawarī, Jahshiyārī, Ibn al-Jawzī) with a level of organizational detail unavailable for any previous Mahdī movement. The matching hadith and fitan material uses the same vocabulary, geography, color-symbolism, and mobilization command as the daʿwa it sacralizes. The match is too specific and too multidimensional to be incidental.</p>

    <ClaimBox title="Central Claim">
      <p>The Abbasid black-banner cluster is best read as the prophetic-form afterlife of the Abbasid daʿwa. Some underlying motifs may have circulated in broader Hāshimite, Alid, or eastern-army apocalyptic environments earlier. But the cluster as we have it was assembled, expanded, and propagated in a transmission environment that overlaps geographically and chronologically with the Abbasid revolutionary network. The Abbasid movement did not invent the black-banner imagery; it captured and redirected an existing Hāshimite-eschatological field by attaching its own organizational machinery to it.</p>
    </ClaimBox>

    <p>Following Motzki, Schacht, and Juynboll, the methodological question is not whether the Prophet predicted black banners from Khurāsān. The methodological question is what these reports were doing at the moment of their earliest historical visibility — overwhelmingly the late Umayyad and early Abbasid periods — and which transmitter networks carried them.</p>

    <p>The chapter's analytical task is therefore double. The first task is to map the eight matn families and their isnād structures against the historical daʿwa material, demonstrating the correspondence between hadith vocabulary and revolutionary machinery. The second is to identify, for each report family, who the Mahdī figure is: explicitly named, deliberately unnamed, retrospectively reassigned, or ambiguously available for multiple Hāshimite candidates to claim. The candidate-identification question is not a side issue. It is the cluster's organizing political problem. The reports' variability on this point is precisely what made the cluster useful to the Abbasid daʿwa, whose own foundational slogan — <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad wa-lā yusammī aḥadan</em> — was a programmatic refusal to name the candidate.</p>

    <p>The pro-banner and anti-banner halves also require analytical separation. Where the pro-banner material commands joining the eastern army even crawling over snow and identifies <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em> among them, the anti-banner material — preserved by Nuʿaym alongside the pro-banner reports — calls the same banners <em>fitna</em>, <em>misguidance</em>, and <em>unbelief</em>, attacks the <em>Musawwida</em> and black clothing as garments of fire, and warns the faithful to stay on the ground rather than join. The contestation is the cluster's defining feature.</p>

    <h2 id="dawa">The Historical Daʿwa: al-Riḍā, Khurāsān, and the Unnamed Candidate</h2>
    <p>The historical control layer for this cluster is unusually rich. The Abbasid daʿwa is not merely inferred from later reports; it is documented in the chronicle tradition with circumstantial detail. Three principal early historians — Balādhurī, Ṭabarī, and Ibn al-Jawzī — converge on the same organizational kernel. Their convergence is the analytical anchor against which the matching hadith and fitan material is read.</p>

    <p>Balādhurī's <em>Ansāb al-Ashrāf</em> preserves what is plausibly the foundational organizational instruction, transmitted after the alleged transfer of authority from Abū Hāshim b. Muḥammad b. al-Ḥanafiyya to Muḥammad b. ʿAlī al-ʿAbbāsī:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="فإن أباه محمد بن علي قال لميسرة وأبي عكرمة مولى قريش وغيرهما ممن كان مع أبي هاشم بن محمد بن الحنفية، حين عدل أبو هاشم إلى محمد بن علي فأعلمه أنه الإمام: هذا ابني ووصيي والإمام بعدي، فبايعوا محمدا وإبراهيم على ذلك. ووجه محمد أبا رياح ميسرة مولى بني أسد إلى الكوفة، بمشورة أبي هاشم، فصيره صاحبه بها. وكان أبو هاشم قال له: عليك بالكوفة فبها شيعتك وأهل مودتك، واجتنب الشام فليس ببلد يحتمل دعاتك ولا يصلح لهم، وعليك بخراسان. فوجه إلى خراسان رجلا أمره أن يدعو إلى الرضا من آل محمد ولا يسمي أحدا."
      translation="Muḥammad b. ʿAlī addressed Maysara, Abū ʿIkrima the client of Quraysh, and others who had been with Abū Hāshim b. Muḥammad b. al-Ḥanafiyya, after Abū Hāshim had turned toward Muḥammad b. ʿAlī and informed him that he was the imam: 'This is my son, my legatee, and the imam after me. So pledge allegiance to Muḥammad and Ibrāhīm on that basis.' Muḥammad then sent Maysara to Kūfa by Abū Hāshim's counsel. Abū Hāshim had said: 'Hold to Kūfa, for there are your Shīʿa and the people of your affection. Avoid Syria, for it is not a land that can bear your missionaries. Hold to Khurāsān.' So he sent a man to Khurāsān and ordered him: 'Call to al-riḍā from the family of Muḥammad, and do not name anyone.'"
      source="Balādhurī, Ansāb al-Ashrāf"
    />

    <p>The passage links, in a single sentence, the four organizational decisions that define the historical daʿwa: (i) the transfer of Kaysānī-Hāshimid authority into the Abbasid line through the Abū Hāshim succession narrative, (ii) the recognition of Kūfa as the Shīʿī base, (iii) the strategic prioritization of Khurāsān as the recruitment ground, and (iv) the foundational slogan — <em>call to al-riḍā from Āl Muḥammad and do not name anyone</em>. The slogan could absorb Alid sympathizers, Kaysānī partisans, Zaydī expectations, and broader Hāshimid sentiments simultaneously, without committing the movement to a specific candidate until victory made specification possible.</p>

    <p>Balādhurī also preserves the timing-doctrine that situated the daʿwa within an articulated theology of revolutionary opportunity:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="قال الإمام محمد بن علي: لنا ثلاثة أوقات: موت الطاغية يزيد، ورأس المائة، وفتق بإفريقية، فعند ذلك يدعو لنا الدعاة ثم يقبل أنصارنا من المشرق حتى يوردوا خيولهم أرض المغرب ويستخرجوا ما كنز الجبارون فيها. فلما قتل يزيد بن أبي مسلم بإفريقية وانتقضت البربر بعث رجلا إلى خراسان فأمره أن يدعوا إلى الرضا من آل محمد ولا يسمي أحدا، ومثل له مثالا يعمل به، فأجابه ناس، فلما صاروا سبعين جعل منهم اثني عشر نقيبا."
      translation="The imam Muḥammad b. ʿAlī said: 'We have three appointed times: the death of the tyrant Yazīd, the head of the century, and a rupture in Ifrīqiya. At that point our missionaries will call for us. Then our supporters will come from the East until their horses reach the land of the West and extract what the tyrants have stored there.' When Yazīd b. Abī Muslim was killed in Ifrīqiya and the Berbers revolted, he sent a man to Khurāsān and ordered him: 'Call to al-riḍā from the family of Muḥammad and do not name anyone.' People answered him. When they reached seventy, he made twelve naqībs from them."
      source="Balādhurī, Ansāb al-Ashrāf"
    />

    <p>The three-times doctrine matters because it shows the daʿwa already operating with a self-consciously eschatological structure: appointed temporal markers, predicted geography (East to West), and a treasure-extraction motif that maps almost exactly onto the Thawbān Group C report's <em>yaqtatilu ʿinda kanzikum</em>. The daʿwa's own theological framework was apocalyptic before it became hadith.</p>

    <p>Ṭabarī's <em>Tārīkh</em> preserves the mechanical detail of the recruitment process in its most explicit form:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="فلما دخل أبو مسلم حائط مرو أمر أبا منصور طلحة بن رزيق بأخذ البيعة على الجند من الهاشمية خاصة. وكان أبو منصور رجلا فصيحا نبيلا مفوها عالما بحجج الهاشمية وغوامض أمورهم، وهو أحد النقباء الاثني عشر. والنقباء الاثنا عشر هم الذين اختارهم محمد بن علي من السبعين الذين كانوا استجابوا له حين بعث رسوله إلى خراسان سنة ثلاث ومائة أو أربع ومائة. وأمره أن يدعو إلى الرضا، ولا يسمي أحدا، ومثل له مثالا ووصف من العدل صفة، فقدمها فدعا سرا، فأجابه ناس، فلما صاروا سبعين أخذ منهم اثنى عشر نقيبا."
      translation="When Abū Muslim entered the enclosure of Marw, he ordered Abū Manṣūr Ṭalḥa b. Ruzayq to take allegiance from the army, specifically from the Hāshimiyya. Abū Manṣūr was one of the twelve naqībs. The twelve naqībs were those whom Muḥammad b. ʿAlī had chosen from the seventy who had responded to him when he sent his messenger to Khurāsān in 103 or 104 AH. He ordered him: 'Call to al-riḍā and do not name anyone.' He gave him a model and described a quality of justice for him. He called secretly. People answered him. When they reached seventy, he selected twelve naqībs from them."
      source="Ṭabarī, Tārīkh al-Rusul wa-l-Mulūk"
    />

    <p>Ṭabarī's mechanics matter because they are precisely what the matching hadith reports presuppose. The reports that command joining the eastern banners are not commanding joining a generic apocalyptic army; they are commanding joining a movement that operated with named missionaries, dated recruitment campaigns, structured organizational categories, and a deliberate naming policy. The line <em>amarahu an yadʿū ilā al-riḍā wa-lā yusammī aḥadan</em> is the operational principle of which the hadith reports' anonymous "man from Ahl al-Bayt" and "<em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em>" are the prophetic-form expression. The reports do not name the Mahdī because the daʿwa they sacralize did not name him.</p>

    <p>Ibn al-Jawzī's <em>al-Muntaẓam</em> confirms the same mechanics through an independent compiler:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="وكان طلحة أحد النقباء الاثني عشر الذين اختارهم محمد بن علي من السبعين الذين استجابوا له حين بعث رسوله إلى خراسان سنة ثلاث ومائة، أو أربع ومائة، وأمره أن يدعو إلى الرضا ولا يسمي أحدا."
      translation="Ṭalḥa was one of the twelve naqībs whom Muḥammad b. ʿAlī had selected from the seventy who responded to him when he sent his messenger to Khurāsān in 103 or 104 AH, and he ordered him: 'Call to al-riḍā and do not name anyone.'"
      source="Ibn al-Jawzī, al-Muntaẓam"
    />

    <ContextNote title="The Nine Organizational Facts">
      <p>The cumulative historical control material establishes nine facts as the basis against which the hadith material is read: (1) the secret missionary network was sent specifically to Khurāsān; (2) the recruitment was framed as a call to <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad</em>; (3) the candidate was strategically unnamed; (4) the daʿī was given a description of justice as the recruiting argument; (5) the recruitment proceeded in secrecy; (6) the initial cell consisted of seventy responders; (7) twelve naqībs were selected from the seventy as an organizational core; (8) the movement adopted black banners and black clothing as its public symbol; (9) the military execution of the revolution was led by Abū Muslim al-Khurāsānī, who took bayʿa from the Khurāsānī army specifically as <em>Hāshimiyya</em>. Each of these nine facts has a matching feature in the hadith and fitan material treated below.</p>
    </ContextNote>

    <IsnadMap title="Group A — Historical Daʿwa Organization" paths={dawaPaths} />

    <h2 id="banner">The Eastern Banner Hadith: Ahl al-Bayt Suffering and the People from the East</h2>
    <p>The first major matn family in the hadith corpus is preserved through the Kūfan chain Ibn Masʿūd → ʿAlqama → Ibrāhīm → Yazīd b. Abī Ziyād, with proliferation downstream into the canonical and pre-canonical Sunnī collections. The matn structure is consistent across the variants. The Prophet sees young men from Banū Hāshim, weeps, predicts affliction for his household, foretells the arrival of a people from the East with black banners who will ask for justice, be denied, fight, prevail, and ultimately deliver authority to "a man from my household" who will fill the earth with justice. The closing command is the most operationally significant element.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="بينا نحن عند رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم إذ أقبل فتية من بني هاشم، فلما رآهم النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم اغرورقت عيناه وتغير لونه. قال فقلت له: ما نزال نرى في وجهك شيئا نكرهه. قال: إنا أهل البيت اختار لنا الله الآخرة على الدنيا، وإن أهل بيتي سيلقون بعدي بلاء وتشريدا وتطريدا، حتى يأتي قوم من قبل المشرق معهم رايات سود، يسألون الحق فلا يعطونه، فيقاتلون فيضرون، فيعطون ما سألوا فلا يقبلونه، حتى يدفعوا إلى رجل من أهل بيتي فيملؤها قسطا كما ملؤوها جورا. فمن أدرك ذلك منكم فليأتهم ولو حبوا على الثلج."
      translation="Ibn Masʿūd said: While we were with the Messenger of God, young men from Banū Hāshim came forward. When the Prophet saw them, his eyes filled with tears and his color changed. I said: 'We keep seeing something in your face that we dislike.' He said: 'We are a household for whom God has chosen the Hereafter over this world. My household will meet after me affliction, scattering, and expulsion, until a people come from the East with black banners. They will ask for the right and will not be given it. They will fight and prevail. Then they will be given what they asked for, but they will not accept it until they hand it over to a man from my household. He will fill it with justice as they filled it with injustice. Whoever among you reaches that should go to them, even crawling over snow.'"
      source="Ibn Abī Shayba, al-Muṣannaf no. 37727 (par. Ibn Mājah, Sunan no. 4082)"
    />

    <p>The report's organizational fit with the historical daʿwa is precise at five distinct points. The Banū Hāshim youths in the opening scene match the Hāshimiyya as the public family-frame of the daʿwa. The Ahl al-Bayt suffering matches the Umayyad persecution of Hāshimid notables that the daʿwa cited as its historical grievance. The eastern direction matches Khurāsān. The black banners match the Musawwida's public color-symbol. The closing command — <em>fal-yaʾtihim wa-law ḥabwan ʿalā al-thalj</em> — matches the daʿwa's recruiting injunction to join the movement under conditions of hardship. The "man from my household" who receives authority after the eastern army wins matches the <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad</em> slogan's anonymous Hāshimid candidate.</p>

    <p>The same matn proliferates through the Sunan witness of Ibn Mājah, and through the Bazzār, Abū Yaʿlā, Ṭabarānī, and al-Ḥākim parallels. The cluster is concentrated on Yazīd b. Abī Ziyād as the Common Link below the family memory and is propagated through standard Kūfan channels.</p>

    <ContextNote title="Source-critical note on Yazīd b. Abī Ziyād">
      <p>Yazīd is the diagnostic transmitter for this matn family. His standing in the Sunnī rijāl tradition is mixed: generally graded weak (<em>ḍaʿīf</em>), with documented Shīʿī sympathies, and identified as a Kūfan transmitter whose late-life condition was assessed as deteriorating. The analytical observation is not that his weakness disqualifies the report from authentication, but that his transmitter profile matches the matn's pro-Ahl al-Bayt content. A Kūfan transmitter with Shīʿī sympathies is exactly the kind of figure through whom an eastern-banner, pro-Ahl al-Bayt matn would proliferate in the late Umayyad and early Abbasid periods. No claim of partisan fabrication is being made; the more limited claim is that Yazīd is the figure through whom the matn becomes historically visible in the second century.</p>
    </ContextNote>

    <IsnadMap title="Group B — Ibn Masʿūd Eastern Banners" paths={groupBPaths} />

    <p><strong>Candidate identification.</strong> The matn explicitly leaves the figure unnamed: <em>rajulun min ahli baytī</em>. The closing handover is doctrinally important. The eastern army does not itself install the Mahdī; it wins the war, is offered what it asked for, refuses it, and then transfers authority to a man from the Prophet's household. In Abbasid reception this maps directly onto the operational reality of the daʿwa: Abū Muslim and the Khurāsānī army win the war; authority is then transferred to the Abbasid imam (Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Saffāḥ at the moment of victory). The matn's deliberate vagueness is exactly what the daʿwa's <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad</em> slogan required. The report is <em>available</em> for Abbasid claim without being explicitly Abbasid.</p>

    <h2 id="thawban">The Thawbān Black-Banner Mahdī Reports</h2>
    <p>The second major matn family is preserved through Thawbān → Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī → Abū Qilāba → Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ, with downstream proliferation through Sufyān al-Thawrī, ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and into the canonical and post-canonical collections. The Thawbān family is more developed than the Ibn Masʿūd family. It contains an expanded matn structure that adds, before the eastern banners element, a "treasure" scene in which three figures — each <em>ibn khalīfa</em> — fight at "your treasure" without any of them obtaining it. The matn then proceeds to the eastern black banners, the unmatched killing they inflict, and the closing command, which in the expanded form goes further than Group B by giving the figure an explicit title: <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em>.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="يقتتل عند كنزكم ثلاثة، كلهم ابن خليفة، ثم لا يصير إلى واحد منهم. ثم تطلع الرايات السود من قبل المشرق، فيقتلونكم قتلا لم يقتله قوم. ثم ذكر شيئا لا أحفظه، فقال: فإذا رأيتموه فبايعوه ولو حبوا على الثلج، فإنه خليفة الله المهدي."
      translation="The Prophet said: 'Three will fight at your treasure, each of them the son of a caliph, but it will not pass to any one of them. Then black banners will rise from the East, and they will kill you with a killing no people have killed.' Then he mentioned something I do not remember. He said: 'When you see him, pledge allegiance to him, even crawling over snow, for he is the caliph of God, the Mahdī.'"
      source="Ibn Mājah, Sunan no. 4084 (via ʿAbd al-Razzāq → al-Thawrī → Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ)"
    />

    <p>The Sunan matn does three things the Group B reports do not. First, it identifies the figure explicitly as <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em> — both title (<em>Mahdī</em>) and theological designation (<em>caliph of God</em>). Second, it changes the command from joining "them" (the army) to pledging allegiance "to him" (the figure), distinguishing the army from the Mahdī more sharply than Group B. Third, it adds the treasure-and-three-sons-of-caliphs scene as the immediate precondition for the eastern banners. The narratorial aside <em>thumma dhakara shayʾan lā aḥfaẓuhu</em> is a transmissional honesty marker that gestures toward content the chain had partially lost.</p>

    <p>Bayhaqī's <em>Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwa</em> preserves the same expanded matn through the same route, but with a critical addition — explicit naming of Khurāsān where Ibn Mājah said only "the East":</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: يقتتل عند كنزكم هذه ثلاثة كلهم ولد خليفة لا تصير إلى واحد منهم، ثم تقبل الرايات السود من خراسان فيقتلونكم مقتلة لم تروا مثلها... فإذا كان ذلك فائتوه ولو حبوا على الثلج فإنه خليفة الله. وفي رواية ابن عبدان: ثم تجيء الرايات السود فيقتلونكم قتلا لم يقتله قوم ثم يجيء خليفة الله المهدي، فإذا سمعتم به فاتوه فبايعوه فإنه خليفة الله المهدي."
      translation="The Messenger of God said: 'Three will fight at your treasure, each of them the son of a caliph; it will not pass to any one of them. Then the black banners will come from Khurāsān, and they will kill you with a killing the like of which you have not seen. When that happens, go to him, even crawling over snow, for he is the caliph of God.' In the transmission of Ibn ʿAbdān: 'Then the black banners will come and kill you with a killing no people have killed; then the caliph of God, the Mahdī, will come. When you hear of him, go to him and pledge allegiance to him, for he is the caliph of God, the Mahdī.'"
      source="Bayhaqī, Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwa"
    />

    <p>Bayhaqī appends the critical methodological note:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="تفرد به عبد الرزاق عن الثوري."
      translation="ʿAbd al-Razzāq alone transmitted it from al-Thawrī."
      source="Bayhaqī, Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwa (critical note)"
    />

    <p>And of the parallel route:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="وروي من وجه آخر عن أبي قلابة وليس بالقوي."
      translation="It is transmitted from another route from Abū Qilāba, but it is not strong."
      source="Bayhaqī, Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwa (critical note)"
    />

    <p>Bayhaqī's caution is a piece of source-critical evidence preserved at the moment of canonical reception. The expanded matn rests on a single ʿAbd al-Razzāq transmission from al-Thawrī; the parallel route from Abū Qilāba is judged weak. Bayhaqī does not reject the report — he preserves it — but the explicit note documents that even the early Sunnī critical tradition recognized the expanded form's narrow transmission base. The implication is that the expanded Thawbān form may be the developed and expanded version of a shorter underlying kernel, with the elaboration concentrated in the ʿAbd al-Razzāq generation.</p>

    <ContextNote title="Candidate identification — Group C">
      <p>The matn explicitly names the figure <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em>. The title <em>Khalīfat Allāh</em> is theologically significant: it is the strongest possible eschatological-political designation, identifying the figure not merely as a Mahdī from the household but as God's caliph on earth. In Abbasid usage this title acquires particular resonance. The Abbasid regnal-name system included a caliph who took the regnal title <em>al-Mahdī</em> (Muḥammad b. al-Manṣūr, r. 158–169/775–785), and Abbasid panegyric explicitly applied the <em>Khalīfat Allāh</em> title to the Abbasid caliph. The matn is structurally available for both readings: (i) as a prediction of the Khurāsānī-led delivery of authority to the Abbasid imam (al-Saffāḥ first, al-Manṣūr consolidator), and (ii) as a prediction of the specific Abbasid caliph al-Mahdī b. al-Manṣūr, reread retrospectively in his favor once he took the regnal title.</p>
    </ContextNote>

    <h2 id="iliya">Khurāsān to Īliyāʾ and the Kaʿb-Style Conquest Apocalypse</h2>
    <p>The third matn family is preserved through Abū Hurayra → Qabīṣa b. Dhūʾayb → al-Zuhrī → Yūnus b. Yazīd → Rushdīn b. Saʿd → Bayhaqī. The matn is short and structurally distinct: it omits both the Ahl al-Bayt suffering and the Mahdī-installation elements and concentrates on a single conquest motif — black banners coming from Khurāsān whose advance cannot be stopped until they are planted at Īliyāʾ (Jerusalem).</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="تخرج رايات سود من خراسان لا يردها شيء حتى تنصب بإيلياء."
      translation="Black banners will come out from Khurāsān; nothing will repel them until they are planted at Īliyāʾ."
      source="Bayhaqī, Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwa"
    />

    <p>Bayhaqī's critical note on this matn is one of the most important methodological observations preserved in the entire black-banner corpus:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="تفرد به رشدين بن سعد عن يونس بن يزيد. ويروى قريب من هذا اللفظ عن كعب الأحبار ولعله أشبه والله أعلم."
      translation="Rushdīn b. Saʿd alone transmitted it from Yūnus b. Yazīd. Something close to this wording is transmitted from Kaʿb al-Aḥbār, and that is perhaps more likely. God knows best."
      source="Bayhaqī, Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwa (critical note)"
    />

    <p>Bayhaqī's caution does three things at once. First, it documents that the matn rests on a single Rushdīn b. Saʿd transmission from Yūnus b. Yazīd, which makes Rushdīn the Common Link and concentrates the cluster's evidential weight on a transmitter whose standing in the rijāl tradition is mixed (generally graded weak, with documented memory problems). Second, it documents that a similar wording is preserved as a saying of Kaʿb al-Aḥbār, the Yemenite-Jewish-convert source for much early apocalyptic material in the Islamic tradition. Third, it openly states that the Kaʿb attribution is "perhaps more likely" than the Prophetic attribution — a strikingly direct acknowledgment by an early Sunnī authority that some Mahdī material may better fit Kaʿb-style apocalyptic lore than secure Prophetic transmission.</p>

    <IsnadMap title="Group E — Khurāsān to Īliyāʾ" paths={iliyaPaths} />

    <p>The Kaʿb crossover is analytically significant for the entire black-banner cluster, not just for this matn. Kaʿb is the principal early carrier of Israelite-style apocalyptic geography in which Jerusalem is the eschatological terminus. Bayhaqī's note suggests that at least one strand of the black-banner cluster is best read as a Kaʿb-mediated apocalyptic motif that received Prophetic attribution late. The implication is that the cluster as a whole drew on at least two distinct streams: a Prophetic-form pro-Ahl al-Bayt mobilization tradition (Groups B and C) and an Israelite-style conquest apocalypse mediated through Kaʿb (Group E). The Abbasid daʿwa was able to use both because both supported the same revolutionary geography: black banners from the East, unstoppable advance, divine guarantee.</p>

    <h2 id="short">The Short Thawbān Command and the Developmental Sequence</h2>
    <p>The fourth matn family is a short command form that omits the treasure scene and gives only the eastern-banners-go-to-them command with the same <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em> identification. The short form is preserved through two distinct chains, one weak and one mawqūf, and is significant for the developmental question of how the Thawbān cluster expanded.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="إذا رأيتم الرايات السود قد جاءت من قبل خراسان، فأتوها؛ فإن فيها خليفة الله المهدي."
      translation="When you see the black banners coming from Khurāsān, go to them, for among them is the caliph of God, the Mahdī."
      source="Aḥmad, Musnad no. 22387 (via Sharīk → ʿAlī b. Zayd b. Judʿān)"
    />

    <p>The chain is methodologically weak: Sharīk (with his late-life memory issues) and ʿAlī b. Zayd b. Judʿān (graded weak in the rijāl tradition) together make this an unreliable bypass route. The matn's interest does not lie in the chain's strength but in the fact that the short command — go to the banners, the Mahdī is among them — circulated independently of the expanded treasure form. The short command is structurally simpler and could plausibly represent an earlier kernel that was later expanded with the <em>yaqtatilu ʿinda kanzikum</em> prologue.</p>

    <p>Bayhaqī also preserves a critically important <em>mawqūf</em> form transmitted through ʿAbd al-Wahhāb b. ʿAṭāʾ → Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ → Abū Qilāba → Abū Asmāʾ → Thawbān, presented as a Companion-level saying rather than a Prophetic report:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="ورواه عبد الوهاب بن عطاء عن خالد الحذاء عن أبي قلابة عن أبي أسماء عن ثوبان موقوفا قال: إذا رأيتم الرايات السود خرجت من قبل خراسان فآتوها فإن فيها خليفة الله المهدي."
      translation="ʿAbd al-Wahhāb b. ʿAṭāʾ transmitted it from Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ, from Abū Qilāba, from Abū Asmāʾ, from Thawbān as a mawqūf report: 'When you see the black banners coming out from Khurāsān, go to them, for among them is the caliph of God, the Mahdī.'"
      source="Bayhaqī, Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwa (mawqūf preservation)"
    />

    <ClaimBox title="Developmental Sequence">
      <p>The mawqūf preservation is the cluster's most diagnostically valuable single piece of evidence. The same matn that appears as Prophetic in Aḥmad's Musnad appears as a Companion-level saying through a related route. Following Schacht's classic observation, this is the textual profile of a saying that may have circulated as a Companion-level statement before being raised to Prophetic status in some transmission lines. The hypothetical sequence: (1) short command kernel circulating as mobilization slogan, plausibly attached to Thawbān as a Companion-level saying; (2) mawqūf preservation through ʿAbd al-Wahhāb b. ʿAṭāʾ; (3) marfūʿ elevation through Sharīk → ʿAlī b. Zayd; (4) expanded form with the treasure-and-three-sons-of-caliphs prologue retrofitted, primarily through ʿAbd al-Razzāq → al-Thawrī → Khālid.</p>
    </ClaimBox>

    <IsnadMap title="Groups C & D — The Thawbān Cluster" paths={thawbanPaths} />

    <h2 id="explicit">Explicit Abbasid Black-Banner Reports</h2>
    <p>The fifth major family makes the Abbasid identification openly. These reports name <em>Banū al-ʿAbbās</em>, name the Abbasid state, identify the Persians as the army-base of the movement, and treat black banners as the dynasty's legitimating symbol. The transmission environment is concentrated in Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād's <em>Kitāb al-Fitan</em>, supplemented by Ibn ʿAsākir's <em>Tārīkh Dimashq</em>, with Kaʿb al-Aḥbār appearing repeatedly as the cited source.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="حدثنا أبو المغيرة عن ابن عياش عمن حدثه عن كعب قال: تظهر رايات سود لبني العباس حتى ينزلوا الشام، ويقتل الله على أيديهم كل جبار عنيد أو عدو لهم. يرابط بساحتهم آدم خمسة وأربعين صباحا، فيدخلها سبعون ألفا شعارهم فيها: أمت أمت. ثم تضع الحرب أوزارها، فيمكث ملكهم تسع في سبع، ثم ينتكث أمرهم بعد ثلاث وسبعين سنة."
      translation="Kaʿb said: 'Black banners will appear for Banū al-ʿAbbās until they descend upon Syria. God will kill by their hands every stubborn tyrant or enemy of theirs. A dark-complexioned man will remain at their courtyard for forty-five mornings, and seventy thousand will enter it, their slogan being: Kill, kill. Then war will lay down its burdens. Their kingdom will remain nine in seven, then their affair will unravel after seventy-three years.'"
      source="Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād, Kitāb al-Fitan no. 570"
    />

    <p>The Kaʿb attribution is decisive for reading this matn. The Abbasid revolution is presented openly as the fulfillment of a Kaʿb-style apocalyptic prediction. The numerology is suggestive: the Abbasid dynasty's foundation in 132/750 plus seventy-three years would place its predicted unraveling around 205/820 — well within the period of the early ʿAbbāsid civil wars (the fitna between al-Amīn and al-Maʾmūn, 195–198/811–813). Whether this is post-eventum prediction or schematic apocalyptic numerology cannot be settled on internal evidence alone, but the report's circulation through Nuʿaym in the early ninth century places its visible reception in exactly the period to which its numbers point.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="وسيكون لبني العباس راية، فمن تبعها رشد، ومن تخلف عنها هلك، ولم يخرج الإمام منهم إلى غيرهم."
      translation="Banū al-ʿAbbās will have a banner. Whoever follows it is rightly guided, and whoever stays behind from it perishes. The imam will not go out from them to anyone else."
      source="Ibn ʿAsākir, Tārīkh Dimashq"
    />

    <p>The matn does three things explicitly: it makes following the Abbasid banner the criterion of guidance, it makes refusing it the cause of destruction, and it locks the imamate within the Abbasid family. This is direct dynastic propaganda, indistinguishable in function from the panegyric prose that accompanied the Abbasid court's self-presentation.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="قال ابن عباس: إذا رأيتم الرايات السود تجيء من قبل المشرق فأكرموا الفرس فإن دولتنا فيهم."
      translation="Ibn ʿAbbās said: 'When you see the black banners coming from the East, honor the Persians, for our state is among them.'"
      source="Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād, Kitāb al-Fitan"
    />

    <p>The matn explicitly links four elements that the historical record links: black banners, the East, the Persians, and "our state" (<em>dawlatunā</em>). The Persian dimension is the daʿwa's actual social base. The Ibn ʿAbbās attribution is post-eventum at this level: the eponymous ancestor of the Abbasid house is being made to predict the dynasty he was claimed by.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="عن محمد بن الحنفية قال: تخرج راية سوداء من خراسان لبني العباس."
      translation="Muḥammad b. al-Ḥanafiyya said: 'A black banner will come out from Khurāsān for Banū al-ʿAbbās.'"
      source="Ibn Hammād al-Khuzāʿī, Kitāb al-Fitan"
    />

    <p>The attribution is significant because it routes the Abbasid black-banner prediction through the very figure the Kaysānī cluster had identified as the Mahdī. The transfer narrative of the Abbasid daʿwa — Abū Hāshim b. Muḥammad b. al-Ḥanafiyya transferring authority to Muḥammad b. ʿAlī al-ʿAbbāsī — is here being reinforced at the prophetic-prediction level: Ibn al-Ḥanafiyya himself, the Kaysānī Mahdī, is made to predict the Abbasid banner. This is the cluster's most direct example of the Abbasid capture of Kaysānī authority.</p>

    <IsnadMap title="Group F — Explicit Abbasid Identification" paths={explicitAbbasidPaths} />

    <h2 id="seeks">Khurāsān Searches for the Mahdī</h2>
    <p>The sixth family integrates Khurāsān into the broader Sufyānī-Mahdī apocalyptic narrative. Where Groups B–D presented the eastern army as the Mahdī's installer and Groups E–F made it the Abbasid dynasty's military base, Group G presents the Khurāsānī forces as actively <em>searching for</em> and <em>supporting</em> a Mahdī who is figured as someone else — a distinct figure not collapsed into either the army or the Abbasid line.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="عن علي قال: يظهر السفياني على الشام، ثم يكون بينهم وقعة بقرقيسيا حتى يشبع طير السماء وسباع الأرض من جيفهم، ثم يفتق عليهم فتق من خلفهم، فيقبل طائفة منهم يدخلوا أرض خراسان، وتقبل خيل السفياني في طلب أهل خراسان، فيقتلون شيعة آل محمد بالكوفة، ثم يخرج أهل خراسان في طلب المهدي."
      translation="ʿAlī said: 'The Sufyānī will prevail over Syria. Then there will be a battle among them at Qarqīsiyā until the birds of the sky and the beasts of the earth are filled from their corpses. Then an opening will be made behind them, and a group of them will enter the land of Khurāsān. The cavalry of the Sufyānī will come in pursuit of the people of Khurāsān. They will kill the Shīʿa of the family of Muḥammad in Kūfa. Then the people of Khurāsān will go out in search of the Mahdī.'"
      source="Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād, Kitāb al-Fitan no. 881"
    />

    <p>The Sufyānī integration is the diagnostic feature. The Sufyānī figure of the later Sunnī and Shīʿī fitan literature is a Syrian (Umayyad) anti-Mahdī, and his presence places the report within an apocalyptic framework that postdates the original Abbasid victory. The Khurāsānī forces here are not the conquering Musawwida of 130–132/747–750; they are a later eschatological army searching for a Mahdī figure who has not yet appeared.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="ثم يطلبون أهل خراسان في كل وجه، ويخرج أهل خراسان في طلب المهدي، فيدعون له وينصرونه."
      translation="Then they will seek the people of Khurāsān in every direction, and the people of Khurāsān will go out in search of the Mahdī. They will call for him and support him."
      source="Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād, Kitāb al-Fitan no. 882"
    />

    <p>The phrase <em>fa-yadʿūna lahu wa-yanṣurūnahu</em> — "they call for him and support him" — is structurally important because it preserves, in the Mahdī-search idiom, the same vocabulary the historical daʿwa used: <em>daʿwa</em>, supporters (<em>anṣār</em>). The Khurāsānī army does not merely wait; it <em>calls for</em> and <em>supports</em> the Mahdī. This is the daʿwa logic projected into the eschatological future. Group G preserves a Mahdī figure who is <em>not</em> the Abbasid imam and whose appearance is structurally delayed beyond the Abbasid victory.</p>

    <h2 id="counter">Counter-Reports and Anti-Abbasid Memory</h2>
    <p>The cluster's most diagnostically important methodological feature is the preservation, alongside the pro-banner material, of an anti-banner counter-tradition that attacks the same symbols the pro-banner reports sacralize. The counter-reports are not late polemic against the developed Sunnī Mahdī doctrine; they are early second-century material that targets the Abbasid daʿwa and its symbols with specificity. Their preservation alongside the pro-banner material in compilations like Nuʿaym's <em>Kitāb al-Fitan</em> documents that the black-banner corpus was contested in real time.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="إذا خرجت الرايات السود فإن أولها فتنة وأوسطها ضلالة وآخرها كفر."
      translation="When the black banners come out, their beginning is fitna, their middle is misguidance, and their end is disbelief."
      source="Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād, Kitāb al-Fitan"
    />

    <p>The matn is a direct semantic inversion of the pro-banner command. Where the pro-banner reports say "go to them even crawling over snow," this counter-report says they are <em>fitna</em> at their inception, <em>misguidance</em> in their middle phase, and <em>disbelief</em> at their end. The three-stage structure reads as an explicit periodization of the Abbasid revolution and the dynasty it produced: the revolution itself as fitna, the consolidating reign as misguidance, the later phase as unbelief.</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: مالي ولبني العباس؟ شيعوا أمتي وألبسوهم ثياب السواد، ألبسهم الله ثياب النار."
      translation="The report says: 'What have I to do with Banū al-ʿAbbās? They divided my community and clothed them in black garments. May God clothe them in garments of fire.'"
      source="Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād, Kitāb al-Fitan"
    />

    <p>The matn is the cluster's most direct counter-propaganda. It names the dynasty (<em>Banū al-ʿAbbās</em>) and attacks two of the dynasty's signature acts: the division of the community (the Abbasid revolution and its aftermath) and the imposition of black clothing (the Musawwida identity). The closing curse — <em>clothed by God in garments of fire</em> — is the symbolic inversion of the black-garment pro-banner imagery.</p>

    <p>The most analytically sophisticated counter-report is preserved through ʿAlī:</p>

    <HadithBlock
      arabic="عن علي بن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه قال: إذا رأيتم الرايات السود فالزموا الأرض، فلا تحركوا أيديكم ولا أرجلكم. ثم يظهر قوم ضعفاء لا يؤبه لهم، قلوبهم كزبر الحديد، هم أصحاب الدولة، لا يفون بعهد ولا ميثاق، يدعون إلى الحق وليسوا من أهله، أسماؤهم الكنى ونسبتهم القرى، وشعورهم مرخاة كشعور النساء، حتى يختلفوا فيما بينهم، ثم يؤتي الله الحق من يشاء."
      translation="ʿAlī said: 'When you see the black banners, stay on the ground. Do not move your hands or your feet. Then there will appear weak people to whom no attention is paid, whose hearts are like pieces of iron. They are the people of the state. They do not fulfill covenant or pact. They call to the truth, but they are not its people. Their names are kunyas, their nisbas are villages, and their hair hangs loose like women's hair, until they differ among themselves. Then God will give the truth to whom He wills.'"
      source="Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād, Kitāb al-Fitan"
    />

    <ContextNote title="Five Diagnostic Features">
      <p>The matn is the cluster's most precise socio-historical description, with five features identifying the target with uncomfortable accuracy:</p>
      <p>(1) <em>Aṣḥāb al-dawla</em> — "the people of the state." This is the standard Abbasid self-designation. (2) <em>Lā yafūna bi-ʿahdin wa-lā mīthāq</em> — "they do not fulfill covenant or pact." This matches the early Abbasid record of broken promises to Alid allies, most notoriously the killing of Abū Salama al-Khallāl. (3) <em>Yadʿūna ilā al-ḥaqq wa-laysū min ahlihi</em> — "they call to the truth but are not its people." This is the precise counter-formula to <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad</em>. (4) <em>Asmāʾuhum al-kunā wa-nisbatuhum al-qurā</em> — "their names are kunyas, their nisbas are villages." The Khurāsānī revolutionary cadres operated under kunya-pseudonyms during the secret daʿwa phase (Abū Muslim's name itself is a kunya), and their nisbas were typically tied to villages rather than tribes. (5) <em>Shuʿūruhum murkhāt ka-shuʿūr al-nisāʾ</em> — "their hair hangs loose like women's hair." This matches the documented hair-style of Abū Muslim's Khurāsānī army.</p>
      <p>The specificity is too tight to be generic anti-rebel polemic. The matn is targeting the Abbasid revolutionary cadres at their socially identifying features.</p>
    </ContextNote>

    <p>The counter-reports do not identify a Mahdī figure. They identify a <em>target</em>: the Abbasid revolutionary movement, its dynasty, its cadres, and its symbols. The matn's final clause — <em>thumma yuʾtī Allāh al-ḥaqq man yashāʾ</em>, "then God will give the truth to whom He wills" — defers Mahdī fulfillment beyond the Abbasid revolution entirely.</p>

    <h2 id="mechanism">The Mobilization Mechanism: How the Call Actually Worked</h2>
    <p>The cluster's analytical force depends on the convergence between the historical daʿwa machinery and the hadith mobilization commands. The operational vocabulary of the daʿwa itself is preserved across the historical chronicles and matches, term for term, the recruitment language of the hadith reports. The convergence is not generic. It is specific vocabulary attached to specific organizational steps.</p>

