    <p>The cumulative judgment is precise. The Abbasid daʿwa did not publicly identify the Mahdī because ambiguity was politically useful. The slogan <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad</em> allowed the Abbasids to absorb Alid, Kaysānī, and broader Hāshimid hope while insiders directed loyalty toward the Abbasid imam. In the hadith reports, the Mahdī is often deliberately vague — a man from Ahl al-Bayt (Group B), <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em> among the eastern banners (Groups C–D), or simply unmentioned (Groups E and the counter-reports). In Abbasid reception, the practical beneficiary is the Abbasid house: first concretely al-Saffāḥ at the moment of the revolution, then al-Manṣūr as consolidator, and then potentially al-Mahdī b. al-Manṣūr as the figure who explicitly took the regnal title.</p>

    <p>The cluster's political genius lay in this multilevel ambiguity. A pro-Alid sympathizer could read Group B and hear it as pointing to an Alid figure; an Abbasid loyalist could read the same matn and hear it as pointing to al-Saffāḥ. A reader in the 170s/780s could hear Group C and identify <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em> as al-Mahdī b. al-Manṣūr; a reader in the 130s/750s could hear the same matn and identify the figure with whichever Hāshimid candidate had received the banners' allegiance. The hadith vocabulary tracked the daʿwa's operational ambiguity, and the operational ambiguity was the daʿwa's strategic asset. The Mahdī of this cluster is structurally unstable by design.</p>

    <h2 id="transmitters">Transmitter Notes</h2>
    <p>The cluster's transmission profile spans multiple regional environments — Kūfan (Yazīd b. Abī Ziyād, ʿAlī b. Ṣāliḥ, Sharīk, ʿAlī b. Zayd), Basran-Yamāmī (Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ, Abū Qilāba), Syrian (Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī, Thawbān as a Syrian Companion in the standard biographical tradition), Yemeni-Egyptian (al-Zuhrī via Yūnus b. Yazīd, Rushdīn b. Saʿd), and the Ḥimṣī compilation environment of Nuʿaym b. Ḥammād. The breadth makes a single-sectarian-transmitter reading inadequate; what the cluster shows is the convergence of multiple regional transmitter networks around the same set of motifs in the period of the Abbasid revolution and its immediate aftermath.</p>

    <ContextNote title="Diagnostic Transmitters">
      <p><strong>Yazīd b. Abī Ziyād</strong> is the Group B Common Link. His weakness in the rijāl tradition is well attested and his Shīʿī sympathies are documented. His role is best read as a Kūfan transmitter whose pro-Ahl al-Bayt content was congenial to the eastern-banner matn.</p>
      <p><strong>Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ</strong> is the Group C/D Common Link. He is a Basran transmitter of acceptable standing whose role anchors both the expanded Thawbān form (through al-Thawrī and ʿAbd al-Razzāq) and the mawqūf short form (through ʿAbd al-Wahhāb b. ʿAṭāʾ).</p>
      <p><strong>Abū Qilāba</strong> appears in multiple matn families. His position is conduit-level; he is best read as a major Basran node through whom Mahdī-related material proliferated in the early second century without himself being a partisan transmitter.</p>
      <p><strong>Rushdīn b. Saʿd</strong> is the Group E Common Link. His weakness is documented; Bayhaqī's explicit note that the Kaʿb attribution is "perhaps more likely" is the cluster's clearest acknowledgment of cross-tradition apocalyptic material.</p>
      <p><strong>Kaʿb al-Aḥbār</strong> is the cluster's recurring apocalyptic source. The Kaʿb attribution appears in Group F (Nuʿaym no. 570 on the Banū al-ʿAbbās banners) and is suggested by Bayhaqī as the more likely origin of Group E (Khurāsān to Īliyāʾ). His presence documents the Abbasid daʿwa's absorption of Israelite-style conquest apocalyptic into its propaganda apparatus.</p>
      <p><strong>Muḥammad b. ʿAlī al-ʿAbbāsī</strong> and <strong>Abū Muslim al-Khurāsānī</strong> are historical-control figures rather than transmitters. The first organizes the daʿwa machinery (the Khurāsānī missionary, the anonymous slogan, the seventy-and-twelve organization); the second executes the revolution militarily. His elimination by al-Manṣūr in 137/755 generated its own substantial counter-tradition (the <em>Muslimiyya</em> / <em>Khurramiyya</em> / Sunbādh movements).</p>
    </ContextNote>

    <h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
    <p>The black-banner cluster is the first major layer in the Mahdī corpus in which expectation becomes organized revolutionary propaganda that actually succeeds. The Abbasid daʿwa did not merely inherit Hāshimite expectation from the Kaysānī and broader Alid environments; it operationalized it. It took the ambiguous hope for a righteous figure from Āl Muḥammad, relocated it to Khurāsān, organized it through secret missionaries and twelve naqībs selected from seventy initial responders, dressed it in black, and then either generated or absorbed hadith and fitan reports that cast the movement as Prophetic command.</p>

    <p>The Mahdī figure in this layer is intentionally unstable: publicly <em>al-riḍā min Āl Muḥammad</em>, narratively a man from Ahl al-Bayt or <em>Khalīfat Allāh al-Mahdī</em>, politically the Abbasid imam at the moment of fulfillment, militarily prepared by Abū Muslim, and historically realized first in the Abbasid seizure of power under al-Saffāḥ, with later retrospective readings available to attach the title to al-Mahdī b. al-Manṣūr once he took the regnal name. The instability was not a defect. It was the mechanism by which the Abbasids captured multiple streams of Hāshimite hope under a single mobilizing apparatus.</p>

    <p>The cluster's pro-banner and anti-banner halves preserve the contestation of this capture in real time. Where the pro-banner reports sacralize the eastern army and command joining the banners even crawling over snow, the counter-reports — preserved in the same fitan compilations by the same compilers — call the same banners <em>fitna</em>, <em>misguidance</em>, and <em>unbelief</em>, identify the Abbasid cadres as "the people of the state who call to truth but are not its people," and explicitly attack Banū al-ʿAbbās for clothing the community in black garments that God will replace with garments of fire. The contestation is the cluster's defining feature in the period of its earliest visibility.</p>

    <VerdictBox title="Final Verdict">
      <p>This is the moment when Mahdī expectation became imperial propaganda. Before the Abbasid revolution, Mahdī material had collected around figures who had been defeated; after the Abbasid revolution, Mahdī material collected around a regime that ruled. The Mahdī of the Abbasid black-banner layer is whoever the daʿwa's deliberate non-identification could be made to specify at the moment of fulfillment. The hadiths were the daʿwa's recruiting language elevated to Prophetic command; the daʿwa was the hadiths' content made into an army that won.</p>
    </VerdictBox>

